---
title: Id Map (Advanced)
id: id-map
description: "The two ids in TanStack AI and what each one means: threadId is the stable key persistence stores and restores by, runId names one execution. What they mean on useChat versus the generation hooks, how to choose a threadId, and when to read runId."
keywords:
  - threadId
  - runId
  - thread id
  - run id
  - persistence key
  - generation scope
  - restore after reload
  - useChat threadId
  - useGenerateImage threadId
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are only two ids to know, and mixing them up is what makes persistence look\nbroken."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Names"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lifetime"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You provide it"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the thing runs belong to: a conversation, or a generation slot"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"as long as your app keeps using the same string"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"yes, from your own domain"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"one execution: one streamed answer, one generation job"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"minted at the start, dead when it ends"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"no, it is minted for you"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistence stores and restores by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":" is what you read when you\nneed to talk to your own server about the execution happening right now."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you omit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":", the client mints one after the view mounts. That\nephemeral id is the wire thread and the DevTools row for this session. A reload\nstarts a new conversation. Persistence needs an explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" from your\napp."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not pass a separate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" is the only identity on chat and\ngeneration clients."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  fetchServerSentEvents,\n  useChat,\n  useGenerateImage,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function ProductPage({ productId }: { productId: string }) {\n  // Chat: the thread id names a conversation.\n  const support = useChat({\n    threadId: `support-${productId}`,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    persistence: true,\n  })\n\n  // Generation: the thread id names a slot that jobs fill.\n  const hero = useGenerateImage({\n    threadId: `product-${productId}-hero`,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/generate/image'),\n    persistence: true,\n  })\n\n  // Each hook also reports the id of whatever is running right now.\n  return (\n    <p>\n      chat run {support.runId ?? 'none'}, image job {hero.runId ?? 'none'}\n    </p>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"threadid-the-key-everything-is-filed-under","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":": the key everything is filed under"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A record is written "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"per "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and a reload looks it up "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"by\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"}]},{"type":"text","value":". If the string is not identical after the reload, there is nothing to\nfind. Pick it from your own domain, keep it stable, and restore works. Mint a\nfresh one on every mount and nothing ever restores."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"on-chat-the-thread-is-the-conversation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On chat, the thread is the conversation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every run in it contributes messages to one growing transcript, stored under the\nthread id. Restoring means replaying that transcript. Runs are internal detail the\nuser never sees, they just see the conversation."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"on-generation-the-thread-is-a-slot","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On generation, the thread is a slot"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A generation job does not append to anything, it produces one result. So each job\ngets its own record, linked to the thread, and restore hands back the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"most\nrecent"}]},{"type":"text","value":" job for that thread: its status, its error, its result metadata.\nSuccessive jobs for the same thing (the first attempt, the retry, the regenerate\nafter a prompt tweak) all land in one slot, and the latest one is what the user is\nlooking at."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"That is why generation thread ids read like a place in your app rather than a\nconversation:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The thing on screen"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A good "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hero image for a product"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"product-${productId}-hero"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The start frame of a video"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"video-${videoId}-start-frame"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The voice-over of a chapter"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chapter-${chapterId}-narration"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A transcription of an upload"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"upload-${uploadId}-transcript"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The support conversation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat-${conversationId}"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two rules follow from \"one slot, latest job wins\":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two different things need two different ids."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Point a hero-image hook and a\nthumbnail hook at the same thread and each restores whatever ran last, in the\nother's UI."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same thing keeps its id forever."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A regenerate is a new job in the same\nslot, not a new slot. That is what makes a reload land on the newest attempt."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"runid-one-execution","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":": one execution"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A run is everything between one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_STARTED"},{"type":"text","value":" and its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"},{"type":"text","value":". It is minted\nfresh each time and thrown away when it ends. Both kinds of hook report the one\nthis client has in flight, or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" when there is none."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"on-chat-a-run-is-one-turn","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On chat, a run is one turn"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It changes from turn to turn, and the mapping to what the user did is not\none-to-one:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A whole tool loop is one run."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The model calls a tool, you return a result,\nit calls another, it writes the final answer. However many loops the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../chat/agentic-cycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"agentic cycle"}]},{"type":"text","value":" takes, that is a single "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One user message can produce several runs."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Pausing on an\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../interrupts/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"interrupt"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ends the run; resuming continues the same\nturn under a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"new"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":". Approve two tools in sequence and one message has\nspanned three runs. While the run sits paused waiting on that approval, nothing\nis in flight, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"So "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat().runId"},{"type":"text","value":" answers \"what is this client running right now\", never\n\"which message is this\". In a "},{"type":"link","href":"../chat/streaming","children":[{"type":"text","value":"live subscription"}]},{"type":"text","value":", a run\nanother client started is not yours to cancel, so it is not reported here."}]},{"type":"code","value":"flowchart TB\n    subgraph chat [\"useChat, threadId: support-42\"]\n        direction LR\n        c1[\"run r1\ntool loop, one turn\"] --> c2[\"run r2\ninterrupted\"] --> c3[\"run r3\nthe resume of that same turn\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph gen [\"useGenerateImage, threadId: product-7-hero\"]\n        direction LR\n        g1[\"job g1\nfirst attempt\"] --> g2[\"job g2\nretry\"] --> g3[\"job g3\nrunning\"]\n    end\n\n    chat -. \"one transcript, keyed by threadId\" .-> cstore[\"messages store\"]\n    gen -. \"one record per job, newest restores\" .-> gstore[\"generationRuns store\"]","lang":"mermaid"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"on-generation-a-run-is-the-job","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On generation, a run is the job"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One call to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generate(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" is one job with one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":". There is no tool loop and\nno interrupt, so the mapping is exactly one-to-one: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":" is the handle on the\nprovider work currently in progress."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"That makes it the id you hand your own server, because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stop()"},{"type":"text","value":" only aborts the\nlocal stream. It does not stop a video render already burning credits on the\nprovider:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents, useGenerateVideo } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function VideoPanel({ videoId }: { videoId: string }) {\n  const video = useGenerateVideo({\n    threadId: `video-${videoId}-clip`,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/generate/video'),\n    persistence: true,\n  })\n\n  async function cancel() {\n    // Stop the provider job server-side, then drop the local stream.\n    if (video.runId) {\n      await fetch(`/api/generate/video/cancel?runId=${video.runId}`, {\n        method: 'POST',\n      })\n    }\n    video.stop()\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <button type=\"button\" onClick={() => void cancel()} disabled={!video.runId}>\n      Cancel\n    </button>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same id is what a durability log is keyed by, so it is also the right thing to\nput in a log line when you are chasing one execution across your server."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-restore-keys-on-the-thread-not-the-run","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why restore keys on the thread, not the run"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A page that just reloaded has no idea what the last "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":" was, so it cannot ask\nfor it. It does know its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":", because your app derived it from a product\nid, a route param, a video id. So the client presents the thread, and the store\nanswers \"here is what happened in it, and here is the run still going, if any.\""}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Only then does the client tail that run's delivery log. Run ids stay essential one\nlayer down, they are just never the entry point. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../chat/stream-events#threads-and-runs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Threads and runs"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the protocol anatomy\nand "},{"type":"link","href":"../resumable-streams/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resumable streams"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the log itself."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"both-sides-use-the-same-thread-id","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both sides use the same thread id"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistence only works when the client and the server file under the same string.\nOn the client that is the hook's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":". On the server it is the activity's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":". For generation the middleware reads it straight off the activity,\nso there is nothing to repeat on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withGenerationPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  generateImage,\n  generationParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiImage } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport {\n  memoryPersistence,\n  withGenerationPersistence,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { input, threadId } = await generationParamsFromRequest('image', request)\n\n  // No scope, nothing to file the job under, so nothing could ever hydrate it.\n  // Reject instead of inventing an id.\n  if (threadId === undefined) {\n    return new Response('`threadId` is required', { status: 400 })\n  }\n  if (typeof input.prompt !== 'string') {\n    throw new Error('This endpoint accepts text image prompts only.')\n  }\n\n  const stream = generateImage({\n    adapter: openaiImage('gpt-image-2'),\n    prompt: input.prompt,\n    threadId,\n    stream: true,\n    middleware: [withGenerationPersistence(persistence)],\n  })\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The client sends its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" on the wire for you, so the hook side is just the\noption. Chat is the same shape: the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" you pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" is the one\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chatParamsFromRequest"},{"type":"text","value":" hands your route and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" stores under. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./chat-persistence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chat persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./generation-persistence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generation persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full wiring."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-you-can-skip-threadid","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you can skip "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Without persistence, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" is optional. After the view mounts, the client\nmints an ephemeral thread id to satisfy the protocol. That id is also the\nDevTools row for the session. The run works, it just cannot be found again,\nwhich is fine for a one-shot image you show and forget."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Turn "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistence"},{"type":"text","value":" on and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" becomes required, on the hook and on the\nactivity the middleware wraps. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withGenerationPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" throws when neither\nthe activity nor its own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"override"}]},{"type":"text","value":" supplies one. An app that\ncannot name the slot has nothing to restore into."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-restore-does-nothing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When restore does nothing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Almost always one of these:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The thread id changed."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"crypto.randomUUID()"},{"type":"text","value":" or a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useId()"},{"type":"text","value":" in it means a\nnew key on every mount. Log the id on both sides and compare the two strings."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The client and server disagree."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The hook files under one id, the middleware\nunder another. They must match exactly."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You keyed on a run id."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Nothing durable is addressable by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":" alone from\na fresh page load. Restore starts from the thread."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Byte storage is off."}]},{"type":"text","value":" For generation, the record never holds media bytes, so\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":" come back while "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"result"},{"type":"text","value":" stays "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":". Add\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./keep-generated-files","children":[{"type":"text","value":"byte storage"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to get the media back too."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"threadid-the-key-everything-is-filed-under","text":"threadId: the key everything is filed under","level":2},{"id":"on-chat-the-thread-is-the-conversation","text":"On chat, the thread is the conversation","level":3},{"id":"on-generation-the-thread-is-a-slot","text":"On generation, the thread is a slot","level":3},{"id":"runid-one-execution","text":"runId: one execution","level":2},{"id":"on-chat-a-run-is-one-turn","text":"On chat, a run is one turn","level":3},{"id":"on-generation-a-run-is-the-job","text":"On generation, a run is the job","level":3},{"id":"why-restore-keys-on-the-thread-not-the-run","text":"Why restore keys on the thread, not the run","level":2},{"id":"both-sides-use-the-same-thread-id","text":"Both sides use the same thread id","level":2},{"id":"when-you-can-skip-threadid","text":"When you can skip threadId","level":2},{"id":"when-restore-does-nothing","text":"When restore does nothing","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThere are only two ids to know, and mixing them up is what makes persistence look\nbroken.\n\n| Id | Names | Lifetime | You provide it |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `threadId` | the thing runs belong to: a conversation, or a generation slot | as long as your app keeps using the same string | yes, from your own domain |\n| `runId` | one execution: one streamed answer, one generation job | minted at the start, dead when it ends | no, it is minted for you |\n\nPersistence stores and restores by `threadId`. `runId` is what you read when you\nneed to talk to your own server about the execution happening right now.\n\nIf you omit `threadId`, the client mints one after the view mounts. That\nephemeral id is the wire thread and the DevTools row for this session. A reload\nstarts a new conversation. Persistence needs an explicit `threadId` from your\napp.\n\nDo not pass a separate `id`. `threadId` is the only identity on chat and\ngeneration clients.\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  fetchServerSentEvents,\n  useChat,\n  useGenerateImage,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function ProductPage({ productId }: { productId: string }) {\n  // Chat: the thread id names a conversation.\n  const support = useChat({\n    threadId: `support-${productId}`,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    persistence: true,\n  })\n\n  // Generation: the thread id names a slot that jobs fill.\n  const hero = useGenerateImage({\n    threadId: `product-${productId}-hero`,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/generate/image'),\n    persistence: true,\n  })\n\n  // Each hook also reports the id of whatever is running right now.\n  return (\n    <p>\n      chat run {support.runId ?? 'none'}, image job {hero.runId ?? 'none'}\n    </p>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## `threadId`: the key everything is filed under\n\nA record is written **per `threadId`** and a reload looks it up **by\n`threadId`**. If the string is not identical after the reload, there is nothing to\nfind. Pick it from your own domain, keep it stable, and restore works. Mint a\nfresh one on every mount and nothing ever restores.\n\n### On chat, the thread is the conversation\n\nEvery run in it contributes messages to one growing transcript, stored under the\nthread id. Restoring means replaying that transcript. Runs are internal detail the\nuser never sees, they just see the conversation.\n\n### On generation, the thread is a slot\n\nA generation job does not append to anything, it produces one result. So each job\ngets its own record, linked to the thread, and restore hands back the **most\nrecent** job for that thread: its status, its error, its result metadata.\nSuccessive jobs for the same thing (the first attempt, the retry, the regenerate\nafter a prompt tweak) all land in one slot, and the latest one is what the user is\nlooking at.\n\nThat is why generation thread ids read like a place in your app rather than a\nconversation:\n\n| The thing on screen | A good `threadId` |\n| --- | --- |\n| The hero image for a product | `product-${productId}-hero` |\n| The start frame of a video | `video-${videoId}-start-frame` |\n| The voice-over of a chapter | `chapter-${chapterId}-narration` |\n| A transcription of an upload | `upload-${uploadId}-transcript` |\n| The support conversation | `chat-${conversationId}` |\n\nTwo rules follow from \"one slot, latest job wins\":\n\n- **Two different things need two different ids.** Point a hero-image hook and a\n  thumbnail hook at the same thread and each restores whatever ran last, in the\n  other's UI.\n- **The same thing keeps its id forever.** A regenerate is a new job in the same\n  slot, not a new slot. That is what makes a reload land on the newest attempt.\n\n## `runId`: one execution\n\nA run is everything between one `RUN_STARTED` and its `RUN_FINISHED`. It is minted\nfresh each time and thrown away when it ends. Both kinds of hook report the one\nthis client has in flight, or `null` when there is none.\n\n### On chat, a run is one turn\n\nIt changes from turn to turn, and the mapping to what the user did is not\none-to-one:\n\n- **A whole tool loop is one run.** The model calls a tool, you return a result,\n  it calls another, it writes the final answer. However many loops the\n  [agentic cycle](../chat/agentic-cycle) takes, that is a single `runId`.\n- **One user message can produce several runs.** Pausing on an\n  [interrupt](../interrupts/overview) ends the run; resuming continues the same\n  turn under a **new** `runId`. Approve two tools in sequence and one message has\n  spanned three runs. While the run sits paused waiting on that approval, nothing\n  is in flight, so `runId` is `null`.\n\nSo `useChat().runId` answers \"what is this client running right now\", never\n\"which message is this\". In a [live subscription](../chat/streaming), a run\nanother client started is not yours to cancel, so it is not reported here.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    subgraph chat [\"useChat, threadId: support-42\"]\n        direction LR\n        c1[\"run r1\ntool loop, one turn\"] --> c2[\"run r2\ninterrupted\"] --> c3[\"run r3\nthe resume of that same turn\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph gen [\"useGenerateImage, threadId: product-7-hero\"]\n        direction LR\n        g1[\"job g1\nfirst attempt\"] --> g2[\"job g2\nretry\"] --> g3[\"job g3\nrunning\"]\n    end\n\n    chat -. \"one transcript, keyed by threadId\" .-> cstore[\"messages store\"]\n    gen -. \"one record per job, newest restores\" .-> gstore[\"generationRuns store\"]\n```\n\n### On generation, a run is the job\n\nOne call to `generate(...)` is one job with one `runId`. There is no tool loop and\nno interrupt, so the mapping is exactly one-to-one: `runId` is the handle on the\nprovider work currently in progress.\n\nThat makes it the id you hand your own server, because `stop()` only aborts the\nlocal stream. It does not stop a video render already burning credits on the\nprovider:\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useGenerateVideo } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function VideoPanel({ videoId }: { videoId: string }) {\n  const video = useGenerateVideo({\n    threadId: `video-${videoId}-clip`,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/generate/video'),\n    persistence: true,\n  })\n\n  async function cancel() {\n    // Stop the provider job server-side, then drop the local stream.\n    if (video.runId) {\n      await fetch(`/api/generate/video/cancel?runId=${video.runId}`, {\n        method: 'POST',\n      })\n    }\n    video.stop()\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <button type=\"button\" onClick={() => void cancel()} disabled={!video.runId}>\n      Cancel\n    </button>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe same id is what a durability log is keyed by, so it is also the right thing to\nput in a log line when you are chasing one execution across your server.\n\n## Why restore keys on the thread, not the run\n\nA page that just reloaded has no idea what the last `runId` was, so it cannot ask\nfor it. It does know its `threadId`, because your app derived it from a product\nid, a route param, a video id. So the client presents the thread, and the store\nanswers \"here is what happened in it, and here is the run still going, if any.\"\n\nOnly then does the client tail that run's delivery log. Run ids stay essential one\nlayer down, they are just never the entry point. See\n[Threads and runs](../chat/stream-events#threads-and-runs) for the protocol anatomy\nand [Resumable streams](../resumable-streams/overview) for the log itself.\n\n## Both sides use the same thread id\n\nPersistence only works when the client and the server file under the same string.\nOn the client that is the hook's `threadId`. On the server it is the activity's\n`threadId`. For generation the middleware reads it straight off the activity,\nso there is nothing to repeat on `withGenerationPersistence`:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  generateImage,\n  generationParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiImage } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport {\n  memoryPersistence,\n  withGenerationPersistence,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { input, threadId } = await generationParamsFromRequest('image', request)\n\n  // No scope, nothing to file the job under, so nothing could ever hydrate it.\n  // Reject instead of inventing an id.\n  if (threadId === undefined) {\n    return new Response('`threadId` is required', { status: 400 })\n  }\n  if (typeof input.prompt !== 'string') {\n    throw new Error('This endpoint accepts text image prompts only.')\n  }\n\n  const stream = generateImage({\n    adapter: openaiImage('gpt-image-2'),\n    prompt: input.prompt,\n    threadId,\n    stream: true,\n    middleware: [withGenerationPersistence(persistence)],\n  })\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\nThe client sends its `threadId` on the wire for you, so the hook side is just the\noption. Chat is the same shape: the `threadId` you pass `useChat` is the one\n`chatParamsFromRequest` hands your route and `withPersistence` stores under. See\n[Chat persistence](./chat-persistence) and\n[Generation persistence](./generation-persistence) for the full wiring.\n\n## When you can skip `threadId`\n\nWithout persistence, `threadId` is optional. After the view mounts, the client\nmints an ephemeral thread id to satisfy the protocol. That id is also the\nDevTools row for the session. The run works, it just cannot be found again,\nwhich is fine for a one-shot image you show and forget.\n\nTurn `persistence` on and `threadId` becomes required, on the hook and on the\nactivity the middleware wraps. `withGenerationPersistence` throws when neither\nthe activity nor its own `threadId` **override** supplies one. An app that\ncannot name the slot has nothing to restore into.\n\n## When restore does nothing\n\nAlmost always one of these:\n\n- **The thread id changed.** A `crypto.randomUUID()` or a `useId()` in it means a\n  new key on every mount. Log the id on both sides and compare the two strings.\n- **The client and server disagree.** The hook files under one id, the middleware\n  under another. They must match exactly.\n- **You keyed on a run id.** Nothing durable is addressable by `runId` alone from\n  a fresh page load. Restore starts from the thread.\n- **Byte storage is off.** For generation, the record never holds media bytes, so\n  `status` and `error` come back while `result` stays `null`. Add\n  [byte storage](./keep-generated-files) to get the media back too.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Id Map (Advanced)\nid: id-map\ndescription: \"The two ids in TanStack AI and what each one means: threadId is the stable key persistence stores and restores by, runId names one execution. What they mean on useChat versus the generation hooks, how to choose a threadId, and when to read runId.\"\nkeywords:\n  - threadId\n  - runId\n  - thread id\n  - run id\n  - persistence key\n  - generation scope\n  - restore after reload\n  - useChat threadId\n  - useGenerateImage threadId\n---\n\nThere are only two ids to know, and mixing them up is what makes persistence look\nbroken.\n\n| Id | Names | Lifetime | You provide it |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `threadId` | the thing runs belong to: a conversation, or a generation slot | as long as your app keeps using the same string | yes, from your own domain |\n| `runId` | one execution: one streamed answer, one generation job | minted at the start, dead when it ends | no, it is minted for you |\n\nPersistence stores and restores by `threadId`. `runId` is what you read when you\nneed to talk to your own server about the execution happening right now.\n\nIf you omit `threadId`, the client mints one after the view mounts. That\nephemeral id is the wire thread and the DevTools row for this session. A reload\nstarts a new conversation. Persistence needs an explicit `threadId` from your\napp.\n\nDo not pass a separate `id`. `threadId` is the only identity on chat and\ngeneration clients.\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  fetchServerSentEvents,\n  useChat,\n  useGenerateImage,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function ProductPage({ productId }: { productId: string }) {\n  // Chat: the thread id names a conversation.\n  const support = useChat({\n    threadId: `support-${productId}`,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    persistence: true,\n  })\n\n  // Generation: the thread id names a slot that jobs fill.\n  const hero = useGenerateImage({\n    threadId: `product-${productId}-hero`,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/generate/image'),\n    persistence: true,\n  })\n\n  // Each hook also reports the id of whatever is running right now.\n  return (\n    <p>\n      chat run {support.runId ?? 'none'}, image job {hero.runId ?? 'none'}\n    </p>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## `threadId`: the key everything is filed under\n\nA record is written **per `threadId`** and a reload looks it up **by\n`threadId`**. If the string is not identical after the reload, there is nothing to\nfind. Pick it from your own domain, keep it stable, and restore works. Mint a\nfresh one on every mount and nothing ever restores.\n\n### On chat, the thread is the conversation\n\nEvery run in it contributes messages to one growing transcript, stored under the\nthread id. Restoring means replaying that transcript. Runs are internal detail the\nuser never sees, they just see the conversation.\n\n### On generation, the thread is a slot\n\nA generation job does not append to anything, it produces one result. So each job\ngets its own record, linked to the thread, and restore hands back the **most\nrecent** job for that thread: its status, its error, its result metadata.\nSuccessive jobs for the same thing (the first attempt, the retry, the regenerate\nafter a prompt tweak) all land in one slot, and the latest one is what the user is\nlooking at.\n\nThat is why generation thread ids read like a place in your app rather than a\nconversation:\n\n| The thing on screen | A good `threadId` |\n| --- | --- |\n| The hero image for a product | `product-${productId}-hero` |\n| The start frame of a video | `video-${videoId}-start-frame` |\n| The voice-over of a chapter | `chapter-${chapterId}-narration` |\n| A transcription of an upload | `upload-${uploadId}-transcript` |\n| The support conversation | `chat-${conversationId}` |\n\nTwo rules follow from \"one slot, latest job wins\":\n\n- **Two different things need two different ids.** Point a hero-image hook and a\n  thumbnail hook at the same thread and each restores whatever ran last, in the\n  other's UI.\n- **The same thing keeps its id forever.** A regenerate is a new job in the same\n  slot, not a new slot. That is what makes a reload land on the newest attempt.\n\n## `runId`: one execution\n\nA run is everything between one `RUN_STARTED` and its `RUN_FINISHED`. It is minted\nfresh each time and thrown away when it ends. Both kinds of hook report the one\nthis client has in flight, or `null` when there is none.\n\n### On chat, a run is one turn\n\nIt changes from turn to turn, and the mapping to what the user did is not\none-to-one:\n\n- **A whole tool loop is one run.** The model calls a tool, you return a result,\n  it calls another, it writes the final answer. However many loops the\n  [agentic cycle](../chat/agentic-cycle) takes, that is a single `runId`.\n- **One user message can produce several runs.** Pausing on an\n  [interrupt](../interrupts/overview) ends the run; resuming continues the same\n  turn under a **new** `runId`. Approve two tools in sequence and one message has\n  spanned three runs. While the run sits paused waiting on that approval, nothing\n  is in flight, so `runId` is `null`.\n\nSo `useChat().runId` answers \"what is this client running right now\", never\n\"which message is this\". In a [live subscription](../chat/streaming), a run\nanother client started is not yours to cancel, so it is not reported here.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    subgraph chat [\"useChat, threadId: support-42\"]\n        direction LR\n        c1[\"run r1\ntool loop, one turn\"] --> c2[\"run r2\ninterrupted\"] --> c3[\"run r3\nthe resume of that same turn\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph gen [\"useGenerateImage, threadId: product-7-hero\"]\n        direction LR\n        g1[\"job g1\nfirst attempt\"] --> g2[\"job g2\nretry\"] --> g3[\"job g3\nrunning\"]\n    end\n\n    chat -. \"one transcript, keyed by threadId\" .-> cstore[\"messages store\"]\n    gen -. \"one record per job, newest restores\" .-> gstore[\"generationRuns store\"]\n```\n\n### On generation, a run is the job\n\nOne call to `generate(...)` is one job with one `runId`. There is no tool loop and\nno interrupt, so the mapping is exactly one-to-one: `runId` is the handle on the\nprovider work currently in progress.\n\nThat makes it the id you hand your own server, because `stop()` only aborts the\nlocal stream. It does not stop a video render already burning credits on the\nprovider:\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useGenerateVideo } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function VideoPanel({ videoId }: { videoId: string }) {\n  const video = useGenerateVideo({\n    threadId: `video-${videoId}-clip`,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/generate/video'),\n    persistence: true,\n  })\n\n  async function cancel() {\n    // Stop the provider job server-side, then drop the local stream.\n    if (video.runId) {\n      await fetch(`/api/generate/video/cancel?runId=${video.runId}`, {\n        method: 'POST',\n      })\n    }\n    video.stop()\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <button type=\"button\" onClick={() => void cancel()} disabled={!video.runId}>\n      Cancel\n    </button>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe same id is what a durability log is keyed by, so it is also the right thing to\nput in a log line when you are chasing one execution across your server.\n\n## Why restore keys on the thread, not the run\n\nA page that just reloaded has no idea what the last `runId` was, so it cannot ask\nfor it. It does know its `threadId`, because your app derived it from a product\nid, a route param, a video id. So the client presents the thread, and the store\nanswers \"here is what happened in it, and here is the run still going, if any.\"\n\nOnly then does the client tail that run's delivery log. Run ids stay essential one\nlayer down, they are just never the entry point. See\n[Threads and runs](../chat/stream-events#threads-and-runs) for the protocol anatomy\nand [Resumable streams](../resumable-streams/overview) for the log itself.\n\n## Both sides use the same thread id\n\nPersistence only works when the client and the server file under the same string.\nOn the client that is the hook's `threadId`. On the server it is the activity's\n`threadId`. For generation the middleware reads it straight off the activity,\nso there is nothing to repeat on `withGenerationPersistence`:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  generateImage,\n  generationParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiImage } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport {\n  memoryPersistence,\n  withGenerationPersistence,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { input, threadId } = await generationParamsFromRequest('image', request)\n\n  // No scope, nothing to file the job under, so nothing could ever hydrate it.\n  // Reject instead of inventing an id.\n  if (threadId === undefined) {\n    return new Response('`threadId` is required', { status: 400 })\n  }\n  if (typeof input.prompt !== 'string') {\n    throw new Error('This endpoint accepts text image prompts only.')\n  }\n\n  const stream = generateImage({\n    adapter: openaiImage('gpt-image-2'),\n    prompt: input.prompt,\n    threadId,\n    stream: true,\n    middleware: [withGenerationPersistence(persistence)],\n  })\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\nThe client sends its `threadId` on the wire for you, so the hook side is just the\noption. Chat is the same shape: the `threadId` you pass `useChat` is the one\n`chatParamsFromRequest` hands your route and `withPersistence` stores under. See\n[Chat persistence](./chat-persistence) and\n[Generation persistence](./generation-persistence) for the full wiring.\n\n## When you can skip `threadId`\n\nWithout persistence, `threadId` is optional. After the view mounts, the client\nmints an ephemeral thread id to satisfy the protocol. That id is also the\nDevTools row for the session. The run works, it just cannot be found again,\nwhich is fine for a one-shot image you show and forget.\n\nTurn `persistence` on and `threadId` becomes required, on the hook and on the\nactivity the middleware wraps. `withGenerationPersistence` throws when neither\nthe activity nor its own `threadId` **override** supplies one. An app that\ncannot name the slot has nothing to restore into.\n\n## When restore does nothing\n\nAlmost always one of these:\n\n- **The thread id changed.** A `crypto.randomUUID()` or a `useId()` in it means a\n  new key on every mount. Log the id on both sides and compare the two strings.\n- **The client and server disagree.** The hook files under one id, the middleware\n  under another. They must match exactly.\n- **You keyed on a run id.** Nothing durable is addressable by `runId` alone from\n  a fresh page load. Restore starts from the thread.\n- **Byte storage is off.** For generation, the record never holds media bytes, so\n  `status` and `error` come back while `result` stays `null`. Add\n  [byte storage](./keep-generated-files) to get the media back too.\n"}
